{
  "image_name": "1c51f7ea5a1bb34525e02fc32d2fca39.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how signaling through TNFR1 can push a cell toward survival, apoptosis, or necroptosis depending on the protein complexes assembled around RIPK1. After TNF binds TNFR1 at the plasma membrane, a membrane-associated complex containing TRADD, RIPK1, TRAF proteins, and cIAP1/2 forms. In the left scheme, ubiquitinated RIPK1 promotes TAK1 and the IKK complex, leading to NF-κB activation, nuclear transcription, and expression of pro-survival factors such as A20 and FLIP. Under these conditions, caspase 8 activity can drive apoptotic execution through caspases 3 and 7, while RIPK1 can also be targeted for degradation.\n\nIf RIPK1 signaling shifts away from the pro-survival branch, cytosolic death complexes form. A DISC-like complex containing FADD, RIPK1, RIPK3, FLIP, and caspase 8 supports apoptosis, whereas caspase 8 inhibition favors assembly of a RIPK1–RIPK3 “necrosome.” This activates MLKL, which then appears to disrupt the membrane and ion balance, producing necroptosis.\n\nThe right scheme shows that inhibiting cIAPs with SMAC mimetics changes TNFR signaling: NIK and noncanonical NF-κB become activated, and a “ripoptosome” containing RIPK1, RIPK3, FADD, FLIP, and caspase 8 promotes apoptosis. A small inset indicates that in unstimulated cells, cIAPs normally keep NIK under proteasomal control.",
